{"unit_id": "Neferuni", "choices": [], "reasoning": "Record 1, Neferuni, reads most naturally as Neferu-ni: nfr.w ('beauty', with the plural ending .w heard as -u) followed by n.j ('belongs to me'). No candidate is made of those words. C1 Nfr-wn.t is nfr 'good' + wn.t, rendered Nefer-wenet. The letters can technically be fitted (w as u, n, an added i, feminine t dropped), but that splits the name as Nefer-un-i and leaves the record's -u- standing for the w of a different word wn.t. The record's Neferu is plainly nfr.w, so the words differ: not the same. C2 Nfr-nw-j, 'Nefer-nu-i', has the order n-w-j after nfr. Rendering it Neferuni would need the record's u to be an added vowel and the candidate's w to vanish, which inverts the 'nu' the translation shows. Different words: not the same. C3 Nfr-jwn.w, 'Nefer-iunu', is nfr + jwn.w (Heliopolis). Matching it would need the final w rendered as i, which is not how the word Iunu is rendered, and the record shows no Iunu. Not the same. C4 Nfr.w-Nj.t, 'Neferu-Neith', does share nfr.w = Neferu. Its second word, though, is the goddess Neith (Nj.t), which modern spelling renders Neith or Nit, not -ni. Reading the record's -ni as Neith with its t dropped would be a different word from the n.j the record renders. This is the closest candidate, but it is still judged a different name. No candidate matches."}
```

